---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';

const title = 'How to Set Up 2FA on Amazon (Authenticator App, No SMS)';
const description =
  'Turn on Amazon Two-Step Verification with an authenticator app instead of SMS codes. Find the setup key behind the QR and generate live 6-digit TOTP codes in your browser.';
---

<GuideLayout
  serviceName="Amazon"
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/guides/amazon/"
  datePublished="2026-11-18"
  dateModified="2026-11-23"
  lastReviewed="2026-11-23"
  sources={[
    {
      label: 'Amazon Customer Service — What is Two-Step Verification?',
      href: 'https://www.amazon.com/gp/help/customer/display.html?nodeId=G3PWZPU52FKN7PW4',
      kind: 'official',
    },
    {
      label: 'Amazon Customer Service — Recover Your Account after Two-Step Verification Fails',
      href: 'https://www.amazon.com/gp/help/customer/display.html?nodeId=GU3SL3GTHLHPDQ2H',
      kind: 'official',
    },
    {
      label: 'RFC 6238 — TOTP: Time-Based One-Time Password Algorithm',
      href: 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6238',
      kind: 'independent',
    },
  ]}
  intro="Amazon calls it Two-Step Verification (2SV) and lets you receive the code by text message or from an authenticator app. The shopping account is separate from AWS — this guide covers the retail login, with a pointer to the IAM guide at the end."
  minutes={4}
  params={['6 digits', '30 s period', 'SHA-1']}
  ctaTitle="Your order history is also your address book"
  ctaBody="An Amazon account holds saved addresses, payment methods, and order history, and it can be used to impersonate you with customer service. Save the Base32 key and any backup codes before you finish setup."
  deepDive={{
    heading: "Amazon account details",
    blocks: [
      {
        title: 'Amazon retail and AWS are separate accounts',
        body: 'Two-Step Verification on amazon.com protects shopping, Prime, Kindle and the retail account. AWS uses its own IAM users and root account with a separate MFA setup — enabling one does not enable the other. If you are here for AWS, use the dedicated AWS IAM guide instead; the keys and consoles are unrelated.',
      },
      {
        title: 'The authenticator option is easy to miss',
        body: 'Amazon may steer accounts toward text messages, and the authenticator choice can sit behind “Add new app” or “More options”. Look for “Authenticator App” rather than “Phone number”. Once enrolled, you can remove the phone method if you prefer not to keep a SIM-swap lane open — but consider keeping a recovery number that is not your main SIM.',
      },
      {
        title: 'What the help page documents, and what it leaves to the screen',
        body: 'Amazon’s Two-Step Verification help page documents the path — Login & security → Edit → Get Started — and the choice between text-message and authenticator-app codes. It does not document the manual-entry link or the backup-code screen, so those are described here by function rather than quoted as labels. The mechanics themselves are standards, not vendor choices: the authenticator option issues a secret that any RFC 6238 implementation turns into the same 6-digit code.',
      },
      {
        title: 'TV devices and household sharing',
        body: 'Fire TV, Kindle and other signed-in devices may keep their sessions, but some will ask for the code again after a security change. If a household member uses the account, make sure they can reach you for a code or have their own profile — and never share the Base32 key itself.',
      },
    ],
  }}
  steps={[
    {
      title: 'Open Login & security',
      body: 'Sign in at amazon.com, open “Account & Lists” → “Account”, then select “Login & security”. Amazon’s help page documents that path for both enabling and disabling 2SV.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Open Two-Step Verification settings',
      body: 'Select “Edit” beside “Two-Step Verification (2SV) Settings”, then “Get Started”. The prompts offer text message or authenticator app — choose the authenticator app.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Reveal the key',
      body: 'Amazon shows a QR code during setup. If your screen offers a manual-entry link — often “Can’t scan the barcode?” — use it to display the alphanumeric setup key. Otherwise screenshot the QR and paste the image into the generator.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Generate the first code',
      body: 'Paste the key into 2facounter.com. A live 6-digit code appears and refreshes every 30 seconds; the calculation runs entirely in your browser.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Confirm the code with Amazon',
      body: 'Enter the current code in Amazon’s verification box and save. Amazon then asks for a code at sign-in, which is how you know the authenticator is the active method.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Save the backup route',
      body: 'Store the Base32 key in your encrypted vault, and save any backup codes Amazon shows. Keep a phone number on the account as a fallback you control, and treat it as a lane you have chosen rather than one you forgot about.',
    },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Does Amazon use standard TOTP?',
      a: 'Yes. Amazon’s authenticator-app option is standard 6-digit, 30-second TOTP with SHA-1, so the key works in any compatible authenticator — including this browser generator.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Does this protect AWS too?',
      a: 'No. AWS IAM is a separate system with its own MFA devices. Follow the AWS guide to assign a virtual MFA device to the root account and each IAM user; Amazon shopping 2SV does not carry over.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why does Amazon keep asking for a code on my TV?',
      a: 'Some devices re-authenticate after a security change or when their session expires. Generate the code on your phone or laptop, enter it on the device, and the session should persist again. The key itself stays in your vault, not on the TV.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Can Amazon customer service bypass 2FA for me?',
      a: 'No, and Amazon documents why: its recovery page says the company is “unable to make changes to your Two-Step Verification settings until your identity is successfully verified”. The documented route back in is the sign-in screen’s “Didn’t Receive the Code” → “Two-Step Verification Account Recovery” flow. Save the key and any backup codes before you need it.',
    },
  ]}
/>
